<!--Post: 250--><!--Time: 1086912140-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST250"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:webmaster@catchbtk.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Tom_voigt<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Tom_voigt<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, June 10, 2004 - 5:02 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Here&#39;s a link: <BR><a href="http://www.kake.com/home/headlines/824732.html"> <b>BTK Back</b></a><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 250-->
<!--Post: 254--><!--Time: 1086964651-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST254"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Jenkins<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Jenkins<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->6-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, June 11, 2004 - 7:37 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Nice to know they haven&#39;t just stopped the investigation, and that they are still coming up with new areas to dig into. I wonder why they didn&#39;t investigate this folk music class long ago? I believe I heard some discussion of this class over on C&amp;J a few months ago---before I gave up hope on decent convo over there. It seems I recall something about the song he wrote about one of the victims &#40;Fox?&#41; was a play on a song from that folk class. Anybody know when this particular chapter of the investigation began? I mean investigating that class, because to me it seems as though after the last press conference they just dropped the ball. However if this all has taken place since then it will restore my ever small bit of faith in the WPD. <BR> <BR> <BR>&#40;what can I say after being handcuffed and taken from my home on a case of mistaken identity when the cop had my ID in his hand at the time and could have prevented the ordeal to begin with I have lost much hope for those boys in blue, i do appreciate them though&#41;<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 254-->
<!--Post: 268--><!--Time: 1087018439-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST268"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Unsub<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Unsub<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, June 11, 2004 - 10:33 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->In the Fox letter there was a poem/song called Oh Death. This poem came from an American Folklore or something like that class, I am sure the same one they are looking at now. The checked it out back in the day but didnt have DNA. Someone on another board said it was a large lecture style class.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 268-->
<!--Post: 270--><!--Time: 1087091309-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST270"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Doone<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Doone<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, June 12, 2004 - 6:48 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Hi So are we talking folk music or poetry here. <BR>Seems like there is a heck of a difference between the two.  But if the reference made by BTK is solidly associated with said class it is great news that detectives are finding the class members and swabbing as well as re investigating <BR>members cause theyve probably talked to BTK already.  <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 270-->
<!--Post: 291--><!--Time: 1087240189-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST291"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Jenkins<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Jenkins<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->6-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, June 14, 2004 - 12:09 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Well I believe it was just a folk music song that had been used in that class. Its not a wildly popular song so its somewhat likely that BTK had something to do with that class.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 291-->
<!--Post: 300--><!--Time: 1087591333-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST300"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Flytodeb<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Flytodeb<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->6-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, June 18, 2004 - 1:42 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->After the KAKE news report of the two students who were swabbed sometime during the first week of June, I ran across an internet article called &#34;Killer caught by relative&#39;s DNA.&#34; It is a very interesting article from the BBC about using DNA to catch a criminal who was not in their DNA data base. Here&#39;s the summary, the link follows. <BR> <BR>This article is about a perverted man in England who threw a brick from a bridge that crashed through the windshield and killed a driver. The brick had blood on it and was tested for DNA but no person in the data base matched it. However, they ran a second DNA screening using the very new technique of familial searching, and a list of 25 people who closely matched the DNA was generated. The man at the top of the list &#40;and presumably the best suspect&#41; was a relative of the man who committed the crime. The police asked the man&#39;s relative--the pervert--to give a DNA sample. Probably thinking the police had absolutely no evidence that he killed the driver, he voluntarily gave the DNA sample. Guess what? His DNA matched perfectly the blood on the brick that killed the driver, and the police had their man.  <BR> <BR>news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/england/3640199.stm <BR> <BR>So, what does this all mean? A son could give a DNA sample and his pervert father could be caught. Or vice versa. If someone thinks a relative could be BTK, he &#40;or she?&#41; can voluntarily give a DNA sample which would closely match the BTK DNA that the authorities have. If that voluntary DNA sample is a close match, then the BTK suspect will be watched and followed at every move. A search warrant and collection of evidence would bring the guy one step closer to arrest and trial.  <BR>-Deb<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 300-->
<!--Post: 344--><!--Time: 1088218101-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST344"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Flytodeb<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Flytodeb<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->6-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, June 25, 2004 - 7:48 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->FYI: <BR>In an online article about how old tactics and new DNA caught the Kansas City serial killer Gilyard, an interesting paragraph you may want to read: &#34;Every week, state crime labs upload DNA profiles from cases to the FBI. Each Friday, those bits of evidence are run against more than 1.7 million samples from convicted felons and unknown offenders, expanding the database with each run.&#34; <BR>Thanks to LE &#40;local, state &amp; fed&#41; <BR>-Deb <BR> <BR>link for the complete article: <BR><a href="http://www.fortwayne.com/mld/newssentinel/8519502.htm" target=_top>http://www.fortwayne.com/mld/newssentinel/8519502. htm</a> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 344-->
<!--Post: 534--><!--Time: 1090382626-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST534"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Flytodeb<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Flytodeb<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->6-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, July 20, 2004 - 9:03 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Tonight&#39;s KAKE special report &#34;Finding BTK&#34; essentially said that DNA &#34;familial searching&#34; is not available in the US and probably would need an act of Congress and acceptance by all 50 states to be instituted. And I was so hopeful about that. Drat. <BR>-Deb<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 534-->
<!--Post: 538--><!--Time: 1090435494-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST538"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Flytodeb<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Flytodeb<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->6-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, July 21, 2004 - 11:44 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Here is the link to the transcript. <BR>-Deb <BR> <BR>From KAKE online <BR> <BR>A new method of researching forensics evidence is receiving global attention. A look at how advancements in DNA analysis could put a 30-year-old killing spree to rest and a Midwest murderer behind bars for good. <BR> <BR><a href="http://www.kake.com/home/headlines/881086.html" target=_top>http://www.kake.com/home/headlines/881086.html</a> <BR> <BR> <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 538-->
<!--Post: 539--><!--Time: 1090437304-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST539"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Flytodeb<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Flytodeb<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->6-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, July 21, 2004 - 12:15 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Sorry about the link not working on the British article above. Here it is again. <BR>-Deb <BR> <BR>The article is called &#34;Killer caught by relative&#39;s DNA.&#34; <BR> <BR><a href="http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/england/3640199.stm" target=_top>http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/england/3640199.stm</a><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 539-->
